cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "CytogeneticsPro: plant cytogenetics in the browser"
abstract: >-
  CytogeneticsPro (from chromosome count to karyotype) is a self-contained web application for
  plant cytogenetics that runs entirely in the browser, with no installation and no server-side
  computation. It measures chromosomes on calibrated micrographs (counting markers, polylines that
  follow the curvature of each chromosome, centromeres, secondary constrictions and satellites) or
  from typed measurements; builds the karyotype by pairing homologs by length and centromeric
  index, averages over cells, classifies each pair by the nomenclature of Levan et al. (1964) or
  Guerra (1986) with configurable limits, writes the karyotype formula, the total length of the
  complement and the fundamental number, and draws the idiogram to scale with satellites, bands and
  in-situ hybridization signals. It computes the karyotype asymmetry indices TF%, AsK%, Syi, Rec,
  A1, A2, A, CVCL, CVCI, MCA, AI and DI and the Stebbins categories; analyses meiosis
  (configurations, chiasmata, recombination index, abnormalities, meiotic index, pollen viability
  with confidence intervals and homogeneity tests) and compares the observed configurations with
  those expected for autopolyploids and allopolyploids, computed exactly from a model of random
  pairing of chromosome ends; estimates ploidy level and genome size by flow cytometry (2C, 1Cx,
  pg and Mbp); evaluates the Allium test (mitotic index, phase indices, aberrations, micronuclei);
  records C, N and fluorochrome bands and FISH signals; compares taxa by multivariate karyological
  parameters, distances, UPGMA, Ward and principal components, and reconstructs chromosome number
  evolution on a tree by parsimony with costs per event; offers teaching simulators and exercises;
  and writes figures, tables, a methods section in Spanish or English and a reproducible package.
  Bilingual interface (Spanish and English). The indices, the classification, the statistics and
  the genome-size conversions were checked against formulas written independently in R (9,310
  comparisons, none failing), and 684 unit tests run in the browser.
type: software
